#hypothesis(title: [Unrefreshing Sleep as Failed Glymphatic Clearance])[
*Certainty: 0.75*

- *Evidence*: Glymphatic literature shows sleep-dependent waste clearance; glymphatic degrades with age @Glymphatic2023DynamicRelationship. Wen et al. (2026) link sleep duration to brain aging clocks @MULTIConsortium2026SleepChartBiologicalAging. The first direct measurement in ME/CFS (Thapaliya et al. 2026, $n = 58$) found a reduced global DTI-ALPS clearance index ($1.44 +- 0.086$ vs $1.51 +- 0.11$, $p = 0.014$) that correlates inversely with self-reported sleep disturbance ($r = -0.47$, $p = 0.013$) and impaired concentration ($r = -0.43$, $p = 0.026$) @Thapaliya2026GlymphaticMECFS — consistent with the long-standing finding that polysomnography shows no clear substrate for unrefreshing sleep @Mariman2013SleepCFS.
- *Mechanism*: Normal sleep clears brain waste overnight; ME/CFS unrefreshing sleep = incomplete clearance → daily accumulation → neuro symptoms + energy depletion → PEM when exceeding toxin clearance threshold.
- *Prediction*: ME/CFS patients will show post-sleep CSF neurotoxin levels (Aβ, tau, α-synuclein) equal to or higher than pre-sleep levels. Successful glymphatic enhancement (improved deep sleep, lateral sleep positioning) will reduce PEM frequency.
- *Clinical implications*: Morning "toxic" headache described by ME/CFS patients (Section on morning headache quality) is consistent with glymphatic failure.
- *Limitations*: Direct glymphatic measurement challenging in humans; most evidence from animal models; the first direct ME/CFS study (Thapaliya 2026) is single-site and cross-sectional (correlation, not demonstrated causation); CSF measurements invasive.
- *Replication status*: Glymphatic field emerging (2012); accumulating evidence in neurodegenerative disease; first direct cross-sectional measurement in ME/CFS now exists but is not yet replicated.
] <hyp:unrefreshing-sleep-glymphatic>
